we begin the day here in europe knowing  more today than we did yesterday about  the new omicron coronavirus variant and  this is when a matter of days matters  today the netherlands confirmed that two  people there tested positive for the  omicron variant more than a week ago now  the world first learned of the new  variant just five days ago and it shows  just how little we really know right now  about omicron  well today german chancellor angela  merkel and the man who will replace her  later this week olaf schultz met with  germany's 16 federal leaders to discuss  how the country should move forward in  dealing with the pandemic no decisions  were announced we don't expect those to  come until thursday and that's despite  the spiraling number of new cases and  the new variant that has been detected  here  for more now i'm joined by paula  picholte she's a member of the german  bundestag for the greens and she's also  a physician it's good to have you on the  program i want to start and ask for your  reaction to this report that is coming  out tonight that olaf schultz set to  become the new chancellor reportedly  saying that he would back mandatory  vaccinations for everyone in germany as  early as february  i think we have to acknowledge that  public support for general vaccine  mandates is surging and also many of my  colleagues are now understanding that we  won't prevent a fifth or sixth wave of  covet infections if we don't have  vaccination rates above 90 percent and  it's quite unlikely that we are going to  have vaccination rates above 90  percent without vaccine mandates and  that's why the discussion about vaccine  mandates  is very likely to be held in parliament  quite soon and is that going to be the  new government stance you're not going  to support new lockdowns but you are  going to support requiring everyone to  be vaccinated  i think it very strongly depends on what  you call a lockdown and whatnot we are  discussing very  very  strict contact restrictions especially  for people who are not vaccinated  but yes it's true  we are going to see if there is a  majority in parliament for general  vaccine mandates and i think the  decision about that is going to be made  in the next weeks  you know we have seen  protests in austria for example on news  of the mandate there  do you expect  to see that type of public outcry  if  the new chancellor says yes we are  in favor of requiring everyone to get  their shots  first of all we do already have  anti-covert measure  measure protests all over germany  especially in  the state where i'm from and saxony and  to all those people who say that vaccine  mandates would lead to further  disruption of society  i say  the current measures the need for  prolonged anti-covert measures because  not enough people are vaccinated  those are causing more disruption in  society than any vaccine mandate ever  could  and is there a plan  if the mandates are  put into force  if people resist  and just say no i will not be vaccinated  in civil disobedience if you will is  there a plan on how to enforce  the mandate  i mean this wouldn't be the first  vaccine mandate in history we've had  vaccine mandates before and in fact we  currently do have measles vaccine  mandates for example for health care  workers so it usually works with  monetary fines and of course people are  not going to be direct to vaccination  places but it's very likely that there  are going to be fines however this is  going to be part of the discussion  that is going to be held in parliament  and you know there are already fines for  people who for example don't get their  children vaccinated  against polio  but you still have people who resist um  do do you think that fines in the  pandemic would be any different or would  be any more effective to get people to  change their minds  i do think i mean we currently we do  have fines for parents who don't get  their children vaccinated against  measles  and um we do 